        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "3015446d-7ec8-5bdc-9c4a-655815a93ecc",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Glazunov, Marius Petipa\n- Works: Symphony No. 9 in D minor, Raymonda\n- Organizations: Saint Petersburg Conservatory, Petrograd Conservatory, Leningrad Conservatory\n- Events: Bolshevik Revolution\n\nFacts:\n- In 1905, Alexander Glazunov became director of the Saint Petersburg Conservatory.\n- Following the Bolshevik Revolution, Alexander Glazunov helped reorganize the institute from the Saint Petersburg Conservatory into the Petrograd Conservatory and then the Leningrad Conservatory.\n- In 1910, Alexander Glazunov began composing Symphony No. 9 in D minor.\n- Raymonda is a ballet in three acts and four scenes with an apotheosis, choreographed by Marius Petipa, with music by Alexander Glazunov as his Opus 57.\n- In 1928, Alexander Glazunov ended his service as director of the conservatory.\n- By Alexander Glazunov's death in 1936, Symphony No. 9 in D minor remained unfinished.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
